<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harikela</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harikela was an early medieval kingdom and region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comilla">Comilla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> in modern Bangladesh. It is known from inscriptions, coins and Chinese pilgrims' accounts from the 7th century, and the Chandra dynasty rose there in the early 10th century. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins circulated widely in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harikela.jpg" alt="Harikela" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P.K.Niyogi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Political_Divisions.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
